# 8 Ball Registry - local development environment
#
# Copy to .env and fill in. This file is committed; .env is not, and must never be.
#
# THE RULE: nothing on this machine, and nothing in staging, may hold production database
# credentials. Production is reached by exactly one command (npm run db:migrate:production), which
# reads PRODUCTION_DATABASE_URL from your shell for that single invocation and nowhere else.

# The local fixture database. Dummy data only - rebuild it with: npm run dev:reset
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:CHANGE_ME@127.0.0.1:55432/8br_dev_fixtures"

# Payload signs sessions with this. Any non-empty string will do locally; it must differ from
# production's, so a session minted here can never be presented there.
PAYLOAD_SECRET="local-development-secret-change-me"

NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Optional. Left unset, transactional email is dormant and messages are written to the console -
# which is what you want locally, because the addresses in the fixtures are on example.test and a
# real provider would try to deliver to them.
# RESEND_API_KEY=""

# LEAVE THIS UNSET IN LOCAL DEVELOPMENT.
#
# Two reasons. The first is safety: this token writes to the LIVE media store, so a local upload
# would land in production. The second is a deadlock - with Blob enabled, Payload stores the file
# remotely and the page then fetches the image back from itself while it is still rendering, which
# on a single-process dev server waits on a request that cannot start until the current one ends.
# Unset, uploads fall back to local disk and both problems disappear.
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=""
